<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    GH-9230: "Expand builtin function recovery to memset / bzero"
    https://github.com/NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra/issues/9230

    Source (foo1, clang-18 -O3):
        void foo1() { unsigned char foo[100]; bzero(foo,100); printf(foo); }
    Compiled: inlined bzero = 6x movaps xmm0(=0),N(rsp) + 1x movl 0,0x60(rsp).

    Stock output: the 100-byte zero fill is emitted as 100 individual char-array
    element stores acStack_68[0xNN] = NUL instead of a single builtin_memset.
    The existing constseq recovery (RuleStringCopy/StringSequence, cleanup group
    constsequence) collapses constant CHARACTER copies into builtin_strncpy, but
    StringSequence rejects a root COPY of zero (a string cannot start with its
    terminator) and formByteArray treats the first zero byte as a null
    terminator, so an all-zero fill never forms a sequence.  The fork's RuleMemset
    (Rec 39) only sees STORE-through-pointer fills, not COPYs into a stack array.

    Fix (GayHydra #477, ported from kuna's memsetrecover / kuna_memsetsequence):
    RuleMemsetCopy builds the StringSequence in fill mode (a repeated single byte
    over a contiguous region, minimum 16-byte footprint) and emits
    builtin_memset(dst,val,len).  Gated by option memsetrecover (fork default
    off = stock output; kuna ships it on, so kuna's script toggled it off for its
    second pass and this script toggles it on instead).

    Pass 1 (default, off): assertion 1 proves the bug reproduces (element stores).
    Pass 2 (option memsetrecover on): assertion 2 asserts the fix emits a single
    builtin_memset call covering the whole 100-byte region.

    Note: parse line extern void printf(char *) is load-bearing; it types the rsp
    buffer as char acStack_68[104], yielding the issue's indexed-store form. The
    printf stub at 0x1000f0 is the target of the relative call in foo1's bytes.
    kuna names the buffer v1; the fork prints acStack_68, so the regexes use \w+,
    and the fork prints a space after each call argument comma (kuna prints none).
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x1000f0">
c3
</bytechunk>
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100200">
4883ec680f57c00f294424500f294424400f294424300f294424200f294424100f290424c7442460000000004889e731c0e8bafeffff4883c468c30f1f440000
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x1000f0" name="printf"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100200" name="foo1"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>parse line extern void printf(char *);</com>
  <com>lo fu foo1</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option memsetrecover on</com>
  <com>lo fu foo1</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="GH9230 #1: default (memsetrecover off) keeps the element-store form (stock behavior)" min="1" max="1">\w+\[0x50\] = '\\0';</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="GH9230 #2: memsetrecover on collapses the zero-fill into builtin_memset" min="1" max="1">builtin_memset\(\w+, 0, 100\);</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
